- op: discuss  # 087b
  at: '2026-08-25T09:21:36Z'  # 087b
  by: agent  # 087b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 087b
  clock: 4  # 087b
  nonce: 087b  # 087b
  message: "DONE 2026-08-25, PR #527 -- and NOT the way this item proposed, because the\nconsequence it predicted does not exist. Recording the refutation, since the\nprediction is the load-bearing part of the filing.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM ASKED FOR: flip load_catalog's missing-file default from\nprovenance_declared to in_progress, having first measured \"(1) which languages\nhit the missing-file path on the corpus, (2) who consumes\ndst_classification_unreliable\". Both were done, and both refute the plan.\n\n(2) THE CONSUMERS. dst_classification_unreliable has exactly one producer --\n_external_potential_chains, io_boundary.py:2370,\n`(catalog.status == \"in_progress\")` -- and one reader, cli.py:5499, which prints\n\"[unreliable]\" beside a caller. The producer is unreachable for a\ncatalogue-less language: the same function skips any source language whose\ncatalogue is `not is_supported` (io_boundary.py:2309), and cmd_io_boundaries\nnever puts one into `catalogs` at all (cli.py:5173). SO THERE ARE NO CHAINS TO\nMARK. The item's sentence -- \"the flip would newly mark every catalogue-less\nlanguage's external_potential chains unreliable\" -- describes a population of\nsize zero.\n\n(1) THE MISSING-FILE PATH, and it IS rarer than the item suspected. 15\ncatalogue files, plus aliases (typescript->javascript, groovy->java) and parents\n(scala/kotlin->java, elixir->erlang, cpp->c) resolving first. Verified end to\nend on a lua+ruby fixture, which is what the path actually looks like in\npractice:\n\n    $ hypergumbo io-boundaries <lua+ruby fixture>\n    Note: no I/O primitive catalog for language(s): lua, ruby. Zero boundaries\n    reported for these languages does NOT mean the code is I/O-free -- it means\n    hypergumbo cannot detect I/O for this language yet. (INV-javam)\n\nThe user already gets the honest disclosure, and it is better worded than the\none the flip would add. Flipping the status would ALSO make\nin_progress_languages warn \"io_primitives catalog for 'lua' is in_progress\" --\na second disclosure saying something FALSE: there is no catalogue, partial or\notherwise. So the proposed change is a regression in output quality.\n\nWHAT WAS REAL, AND IS FIXED. The FIELD lies to whoever reads it. `status` is a\nclaim about a catalogue's provenance and the fallback answered\nprovenance_declared for a catalogue that does not exist. Nothing reads it that\nway today ONLY because both readers ask `== \"in_progress\"` -- a property of\ntoday's two call sites, not of the field. The next consumer to ask\n`== \"provenance_declared\"` gets a citation-backed answer for a language nobody\ncatalogued, and it fails OPEN. That is this item's title, exactly, minus the\nremedy it guessed at.\n\nSHIPPED: the fallback carries CATALOG_STATUS_UNSUPPORTED, a third value A\nCATALOGUE FILE MAY NOT DECLARE -- the YAML validator's existing two-value check\nrefuses it -- because it describes the ABSENCE of the file rather than anything\nthe file says. Behaviour-preserving by construction; both readers asserted\ndirectly rather than inferred, with controls that a real catalogue is untouched\n(python provenance_declared, go in_progress, kotlin inheriting java's).\n\ntest_missing_catalog_default_pinned_as_is was the RED this item nominated. It is\nflipped, and the comment inside it -- which asserted the consequence refuted\nabove -- is rewritten rather than left behind a banner."  # 087b
